# catawiki-scraper — copy to .env next to the scripts and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   python3 env_config.py        # prints what was picked up, WITHOUT secrets
#
# Credentials belong here and never on a command line: a secret in argv is
# readable by anything that can run `ps` and lands in shell history.
# `.env` is in .gitignore; keep it that way.
#
# Precedence, highest first:
#   an explicit flag  ->  an exported environment variable  ->  .env  ->  default
#
# Every value below is a PLACEHOLDER. Anything still carrying `{...}` braces
# is treated as unset, so a copied example is never sent to an API as if it
# were a key — the alternative is a 401 a long way from its cause.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2Captcha — one key, four separately billed products
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Captcha solving, the Scraping Browser API, proxies and fingerprints all sit
# behind this one key: https://2captcha.com
#
# Worth knowing before you buy any of it: an ordinary local Chrome on an
# ordinary connection reads this site fine. What the paid products buy here is
# volume from many addresses, a specific exit country, and no browser to run
# yourself — not access.
TWOCAPTCHA_KEY=your_2captcha_api_key_here

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Scraping Browser API — a remote browser over CDP
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Measured working against this site: HTTP 200 and the full catalogue, from a
# residential Dutch exit. It is also the only path that works headless-like on
# a machine with no browser, because the browser is not yours.
#
#   country-{cc}   picks the exit country
#   pid-{id}       a profile with persistent cookies, ONE live connection each
#                  — reuse pids rather than minting one per run, and expect
#                  `profile_locked` if two runs share one
#
# --concurrency above 1 is refused with this endpoint for that reason; use
# several pids, one run each.
CATAWIKI_CDP_ENDPOINT=ws://{login}-zone-scraping_browser-country-nl-pid-{profileId}:{password}@cb.2captcha.com:9222

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A proxy exit
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read as a single exit, or use --proxy-file for a pool. Two things measured
# on this account are worth repeating, because both cost an afternoon:
#
#  * A proxy does NOT get a headless browser past this site. HTTP 403 from
#    four residential exits and one datacentre address, against 200 from the
#    very same addresses with a real window. The discriminator is the client,
#    not the address, so reach for --headful (the default) before reaching
#    for a proxy.
#
#  * Chromium cannot authenticate a SOCKS5 proxy — Playwright refuses at
#    launch with "Browser does not support socks5 proxy authentication", and
#    Selenium cannot authenticate any proxy at all. The way around both is
#    2Captcha's IP-whitelist mode: whitelist your address, then ask
#    /proxy/generate_white_list_connections for connections and you get one
#    `host:port` per exit with NO credentials in them at all. Those work in
#    every engine, drop straight into --proxy-file, and keep the credential
#    out of your config entirely.
CATAWIKI_PROXY=http://{user}:{password}@nl.proxy.2captcha.com:2334

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A default URL, so a run needs no --url
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Any of the four page kinds:
#   category   https://www.catawiki.com/en/c/333-watches
#   search     https://www.catawiki.com/en/s?q=rolex
#   auction    https://www.catawiki.com/en/a/1243988-figures-figurines-auction
#   lot        https://www.catawiki.com/en/l/106583855-omega-de-ville
#
# An auction page is the best of them for price monitoring: it carries every
# one of its lots at once AND the auction's absolute close time, which a
# category listing does not state anywhere.
#
# Deliberately NOT here: an output prefix. `--out` already carries a non-empty
# default, so the loader would never see it as unset and the variable would be
# silently inert — a setting that looks configurable and is not.
CATAWIKI_URL=https://www.catawiki.com/en/c/333-watches
